    "dwell": {
      "word": "DWELL",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "To delay; to linger. [Obs.]",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To abide; to remain; to continue. I 'll rather dwell in my necessity. Shak. Thy soul was like a star and dwelt apart. Wordsworth.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To abide as a permanent resident, or for a time; to live in a place; to reside. The parish in which I was born, dwell, and have possessions. Peacham. The poor man dwells in a humble cottage near the hall where the lord of the domain resides. C. J. Smith. To dwell in, to abide in (a place); hence, to depend on. \"My hopes in heaven to dwell.\" Shak. absorbed with; to stick to; to make much of; as, to dwell upon a subject; a singer dwells on a note. They stand at a distance, dwelling on his looks and language, fixed in amazement. Buckminster.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To inhabit. [R.] Milton.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
